If there is already a multiple of 3, ans = 0.
If not, we need to increment some $a_i$ until it becomes a multiple of 3.
For a number $x$, the cost to make it divisible by 3 is $(3 - (x \% 3)) \% 3$.
We want to minimize this cost over all $i$.
So ans = $\min_i ((3 - (a_i \% 3)) \% 3)$.

Case $k=5$:
Similar to $k=3$. We need at least one $a_i$ divisible by 5.
If already exists, ans = 0.
Else, ans = $\min_i ((5 - (a_i \% 5)) \% 5)$.

